data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 11, the **sync-over-async opt-in for the EF Core Azure Cosmos DB provider is removed entirely**. The Cosmos DB provider now **only supports asynchronous operations** — there is no configuration flag to restore sync behavior.

Every synchronous LINQ call in your repository (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`) will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

### Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key changes

| .NET 10 (with opt-in) | .NET 11 |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `First()` | `await FirstAsync()` |
| `Count()` | `await CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

This will ripple up your call stack — callers of these methods must also become `async`/`await`. Make sure to propagate `async` all the way to your controllers (which ASP.NET Core already supports natively).
